From 77b5f8c8e44563fdfaefe4e587773235d4b32586 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pieroviano <piero.viano@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 07:24:30 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Arrange docked and anchored children the way WinForms does

The first measurement of the layout engine against real WinForms rather than
against itself - and it found the largest behavioural defect in the library.

Dock and anchor were expressed by handing each child a GTK alignment: a
top-docked child got Halign=Fill, Valign=Start, and that was the implementation.
Alignments do not compose, so two top-docked panels were both aligned to the top
edge and overlapped instead of stacking, DockStyle.Fill covered the whole
container rather than what the other docked children had left, and anchoring was
computed once at realize and never again - resizing a container moved nothing.
Control.DisplayRectangle was an auto-property nothing assigned, returning
Rectangle.Empty for every control, and ScrollableControl overrode it with a
second inert auto-property so Form reported Empty too.

The arithmetic is now WinForms': the display rectangle is the client area less
Padding; docked children consume edges of a shrinking remainder, walked from the
END of the control collection towards the start (so the last-added docks first);
Fill takes whatever is left when its turn comes; invisible children consume
nothing; and undocked children follow the container's size delta by their anchor,
including the rule nobody expects - anchored to no edge means move by HALF the
delta, not stay put. Results are pushed to GTK as absolute margins with
Align.Start, so the widget tree carries positions instead of alignment rules.

The expected values were not reasoned about. Each case was run against real
System.Windows.Forms (net10.0-windows, CreateControl + PerformLayout) and those
rectangles are the oracle, recorded in the test file and in EMULATION-GAPS 4.8.
The dock cases assert through a live GTK allocation; the anchor rule is asserted
as arithmetic because this test host does not propagate a runtime resize to a
modal dialog's allocation - a resize-driven test would have asserted nothing.

Sample-app screenshot baselines updated: the three that moved were inspected
side by side, and the differences are small geometry shifts consistent with exact
rectangles replacing alignment plus its 3px fudge margins. One cosmetic artifact
is recorded rather than chased - an exactly-sized filled child can sit a pixel or
two proud of its GTK viewport and raise a scrollbar WinForms would not show.

